gather corporation details needed for reinstatement calculation.

.why = we need these details to calculate what you owe and prepare for the call to Delaware.

---

## lookup your status

search for your corporation at:
https://icis.corp.delaware.gov/ecorp/entitysearch/namesearch.aspx

### ⏱️ why the clock matters — § 502(d) exposes you to SILENT service of process

> this route treats delinquency as a **money** problem. it is
> also a **notice** problem, and this one can cost a default judgment.

> "In case any corporation shall fail to file its annual franchise tax report
> and the franchise tax due within the time required by this chapter, and in
> case the agent in charge of the registered office ... **shall die, resign,
> refuse to act as such, remove from this State or cannot with due diligence be
> found, it shall be lawful while default continues to serve process against
> the corporation upon the Secretary of State.**"
>
> — 8 Del. C. § 502(d), https://delcode.delaware.gov/title8/c005/index.html

⛔ **read the two conditions — they are CUMULATIVE, joined by "and in case."**

| condition | your position |
|---|---|
| the corporation failed to file its report and pay | ⚠️ true, if you are on this route |
| **and** the registered agent is dead / resigned / refuses / left the State / cannot be found | 🚩 **check this. it is the one that decides the risk** |

⭐ **so a paid, live registered agent is the whole defense** — and a lapsed
agent is exactly what a delinquent corporation tends to have, because the agent
fee lapses for the same reason the tax did.

🎯 **do this now, before anything else on this stone:** confirm the registered
agent on the Division's entity page is current and paid. **if the agent has
lapsed, a suit can be served on the Secretary of State and you may never learn
of it** until a judgment exists.

⚠️ this does not pause the reinstatement work — it runs beside it, and it is the
only item on this route with a deadline set by a stranger.

---

## required fields

| field | your info | where to find |
|-------|-----------|---------------|
| corporation name | | Delaware search |
| file number | | Delaware search |
| date of incorporation | | Delaware search — ⛔ **§ 312(d)(1) requires it on the revival certificate** |
| ⛔ **date voided** | | Delaware search (status as of) — ⛔ **§ 312(d)(4) requires it. see below** |
| ⛔ **name as of the void date** | | Delaware search — § 312(d)(1) asks for the **original** name AND the name it bore when voided. usually identical; confirm rather than assume |
| authorized shares | | Delaware search |
| tax due (their calculation) | | Delaware search |
| annual assessment | | Delaware search |
| registered agent | | Delaware search |

---

### 🚩 the VOID DATE is a REQUIRED FIELD — confirm it, never derive it

> this table listed the void date beside seven other lookups,
> as though a close estimate would serve. it will not.

> "(d) The certificate required by subsection (c) of this section shall state:
> ... (4) **The date when the certificate of incorporation became forfeited or
> void** pursuant to this title ..."
>
> — 8 Del. C. § 312(d)(4), https://delcode.delaware.gov/title8/c001/sc12/index.html

⛔ **the revival certificate is filed under § 103 and is sworn.** a derived date
is a guess in a sworn instrument.

the usual derivation looks airtight and is not: tax due March 1 → § 510's **one
year** of neglect → the mandatory **November 30** notice → void *"on or before
March 1 of the following year."* two things break it:

| what moves the date | direction |
|---|---|
| § 510's **good-cause extension** — *"unless the Secretary of State, for good cause shown, shall have given further time"* | **later**, and invisibly |
| the **unfiled complete report** prong, which bites independently of the tax | **earlier** |

#### ⛔ § 510 IN FULL — because every error about it came from a fragment

this section is cited throughout this route and, until now, **quoted nowhere in
it**. read the whole of it once; four separate misreadings dissolve on contact:

> "If any corporation, accepting the Constitution of this State and coming under
> Chapter 1 of this title, or any corporation which has heretofore filed or may
> hereafter file a certificate of incorporation under said chapter, neglects or
> refuses for 1 year to pay the State any franchise tax or taxes, which has or
> have been, or shall be assessed against it, or which it is required to pay
> under this chapter, or shall neglect or refuse to file a complete annual
> franchise tax report, the charter of the corporation shall be void, and all
> powers conferred by law upon the corporation are declared inoperative, unless
> the Secretary of State, for good cause shown, shall have given further time for
> payment of the tax or taxes or the completion of an annual franchise tax
> report, in which case a certificate thereof shall be filed in the office of the
> Secretary of State stating the reason therefor. On or before November 30 in
> each year, the Secretary of State shall notify each corporation which has
> neglected or refused to pay the franchise tax or taxes assessed against it or
> becoming due during the year or has refused or neglected to file a complete
> annual franchise tax report, that the charter of the corporation shall become
> void unless such taxes are paid and such complete annual franchise tax report
> is filed on or before March 1 of the following year."
>
> — 8 Del. C. § 510, in full,
> https://delcode.delaware.gov/title8/c005/index.html

| the common belief | what the text says |
|---|---|
| "two consecutive years" | ⛔ **"neglects or refuses for 1 year"** — and the section's own caption reads *"…for 1 year"* |
| "no notice is given" | ⛔ **"the Secretary of State SHALL notify"**, on or before November 30 — mandatory |
| "the office has no discretion" | ⛔ an express **good-cause** extension, memorialized by a filed certificate |
| "it is a tax default" | ⛔ **two independent triggers** — unpaid tax **OR** an unfiled complete report |

⭐ **the "two years" belief is the dangerous one, because on an ordinary calendar
it COINCIDES.** one year of neglect after a March 1 due date puts the notice at
November 30 and the void at the next March 1 — two March 1sts elapse, so the
wrong rule yields the right date and is never tested. it fails precisely where
it matters: a good-cause extension moves the date **later**, the report prong can
bite **earlier**, and a corporation that never received the notice has no
two-year cushion at all.

🎯 **get it from Delaware — the entity page, or the franchise tax line.** it is
one question and it unblocks a field you cannot file without.

⚠️ **also confirm the incorporation date against Delaware's record, not your
packet.** the two can disagree by a day when the certificate was executed on one
date and filed on the next; § 312(d)(1) wants *"the date of **filing**."*

---

## additional fields (from your records)

Delaware does NOT have these — you must find them in your internal documents.

| field | where to find |
|-------|---------------|
| **issued shares** | stock ledger, cap table, board resolutions, or stock certificates you signed |
| **gross assets** | ⛔ **not a number you build.** it is the "total assets" line of **Form 1120, Schedule L** for the right year — see below |
| **par value** | ⛔ **read it off the Certificate of Incorporation.** it is stated there verbatim — see below |

### where are these documents?

| document | typical location |
|----------|------------------|
| Certificate of Incorporation | your incorporation service (Stripe Atlas, Clerky, etc.) or your files |
| stock ledger / cap table | **your incorporation service first** (Stripe Atlas, Clerky, Firstbase), then Carta, Pulley, Captable.io, or a spreadsheet you created |
| board consent that authorizes issuance | **the same incorporation service**, or its DocuSign / e-signature archive |
| stock purchase agreement (CSPA) | same — it is in the same envelope as the consent |
| board resolutions | your records, or your lawyer if you had one |
| tax returns | your accountant, TurboTax, or IRS transcripts |

### ⛔ if you can't find issued shares — you have NOT found zero

> ⛔ **"I can't find it" and "there is none" look identical. they are not the
> same fact, and only one of them is safe to report to Delaware.**

if you incorporated through **Stripe Atlas, Clerky, or Firstbase**, that service
almost certainly issued you founder stock at inception, e-signed the board
consent and the purchase agreement for you, and filed both in a dashboard you
have not opened since. so an empty desk drawer is the **expected** observation
even when shares were issued.

| what you did | what it settles |
|---|---|
| searched your own files, found nothing | ⛔ **nothing.** open the incorporation service's dashboard |
| opened the dashboard, found the consent + CSPA | ✅ shares were issued — the count is in **Exhibit A** |
| opened the dashboard, and it is genuinely empty | ⚠️ still not proof — check the § 219(c) stock ledger, then ask the service |

⛔ **do NOT enter a "typical" or assumed share count.** the number you write here
is passed to `franchise.tax.delaware --issued` in step 1 and is **said aloud to a
Delaware clerk** in step 2, about an annual report that carries 8 Del. C.
§ 502(b) perjury. a guess is a false statement with an audit trail.

if you truly cannot establish the count, **stop here and get it.**

### 🚩 but the ledger is NOT the only artifact that can answer — read § 219(c) whole

> 🚩 **this section read *"the ledger is the only artifact that answers, and
> § 219(c) makes it 'the only evidence as to who are the stockholders.'"*
> corrected.** the quote stopped mid-sentence, and the clause it
> dropped is the one that bounds the rule.

> "(c) For purposes of this chapter, **'stock ledger' means 1 or more records
> administered by or on behalf of the corporation** in which the names of all of
> the corporation's stockholders of record, the address and number of shares
> registered in the name of each such stockholder, and all issuances and
> transfers of stock of the corporation are recorded **in accordance with § 224
> of this title**. The stock ledger shall be the only evidence as to who are the
> stockholders **entitled by this section to examine the list required by this
> section or to vote in person or by proxy at any meeting of stockholders**."
>
> — 8 Del. C. § 219(c), https://delcode.delaware.gov/title8/c001/sc07/index.html

⛔ **the exclusivity is SCOPED to two rights** — who may examine the § 219(a)
list, and who may vote. it is not a general rule that no other document may
prove a share was issued.

| the question | is § 219(c) exclusive about it? |
|---|---|
| who may vote at a stockholder session | ✅ **yes** — the ledger, and only the ledger |
| who may examine the stockholder list | ✅ yes |
| ⭐ **were shares issued, and how many** — the § 502(a) report question | ⛔ **no.** the board consent, the CSPA, and Exhibit A are not excluded |

⚠️ **read what the overstatement costs you.** it tells a founder whose Atlas
dashboard holds a signed consent and a CSPA that those documents cannot answer,
so the honest count he already has looks unusable — and the two exits that
leaves are both wrong: **guess** (a § 502(b) false statement), or **stall** the
whole reinstatement on a document he does not need.

### ⭐ and your electronic records may BE the ledger, rather than a substitute

read the definition again: *"1 or more **records** administered by or on behalf
of the corporation."* it does not say a bound book, and § 224 says so in terms:

> ✅ **VERIFIED 2026-09-14** against
> `.scratch/refs/pulls/delcode.title8.c001.sc07.txt` (76,395 chars, pulled the
> same day via bhrowser, headful).
>
> 🚩 **it was UNVERIFIED for weeks, and the pull found a real truncation.** the
> quote used to end at "…would have been." the statute continues "**, provided
> the paper form accurately portrays the record.**" — a **condition on
> admissibility**, and the operative one for the argument below: a dashboard
> printout is evidence only if it faithfully portrays the record it came from.
>
> ⭐ **the dropped clause is a proviso about faithful reproduction, and it was
> dropped by an unfaithful reproduction.** that is not irony, it is the
> mechanism: a truncation at a sentence boundary reads as a complete thought,
> so the eye supplies a period the legislature did not write.

> "Any records administered by or on behalf of the corporation in the regular
> course of its business, **including its stock ledger** … **may be kept on, or
> by means of, or be in the form of, any information storage device, method, or
> 1 or more electronic networks or databases** … provided that the records so
> kept can be converted into clearly legible paper form within a reasonable time
> … a clearly legible paper form … **shall be valid and admissible in evidence,
> and accepted for all other purposes**, to the same extent as an original paper
> record of the same information would have been, **provided the paper form
> accurately portrays the record.**"
>
> — 8 Del. C. § 224, https://delcode.delaware.gov/title8/c001/sc07/index.html

⭐ **so an incorporation service's dashboard is a candidate stock ledger**, not
a lesser record. it is a database, administered **on behalf of** the
corporation, in which the holder, the address, the count, and the issuance are
recorded.

⚠️ **§ 224 attaches three conditions, and two reach provisions this route has
not pulled.** stated bare, because a guess beside an open flag closes the gate:

| condition | status |
|---|---|
| (i) the records can prepare the §§ 219/220 stockholder list | ✅ checkable from the dashboard |
| (ii) they record the information specified in **§§ 156, 159, 217(a), 218** | ⚠️ **half evaluable — see below.** §§ 156 and 159 not pulled |
| (iii) they record transfers per **Article 8, subtitle I, Title 6** | 🚩 **not pulled** |

### ⭐ two of (ii)'s four sections ARE evaluable, and both turn on one question

read together, § 217(a) and § 218 make one demand of your records: **does anyone
other than the registered owner hold the vote?**

> ✅ **VERIFIED 2026-09-14.** both quotes below now check byte-for-byte against
> `.scratch/refs/pulls/delcode.title8.c001.sc07.txt`.
>
> 🚩 **this paragraph read "§ 217(a) and § 218 are on disk" until today, and it
> was false.** a census of the DGCL containers this repo actually held refuted
> it: **subchapter VII had never been pulled.** what was on disk was subchapter
> I (§§ 101–116), subchapter XI, subchapter XII, and chapter 5 — while §§ 217,
> 218, 224, and 228 all live in **sc07**.
>
> ⛔ **"on disk" was a claim about the world with no measurement behind it**, and
> it is the more dangerous half of the pair: the quotes were in fact faithful,
> but a reader who wanted to check could not have, and the sentence told them
> not to bother. ⭐ **a false reassurance defeats the audit that would have
> caught it** — which is why the § 224 truncation two sections down survived
> this long.
>
> ⚠️ the diagnosis came from **where** each quote broke. all of these diverged
> at **char 4–6 of several hundred** — the signature of an **absent container**,
> never of an altered quote. an early break means the matcher found no anchor at
> all; a late break means it tracked the text and then lost it, which is a real
> alteration. reading that number is what separated "go pull it" from "go fix
> it" across ten flagged quotes.

> "(a) **Persons holding stock in a fiduciary capacity** shall be entitled to
> vote the shares so held. **Persons whose stock is pledged** shall be entitled
> to vote, unless in the transfer by the pledgor on the books of the corporation
> such person has expressly empowered the **pledgee** to vote thereon, in which
> case **only the pledgee**, or such pledgee's proxy, may represent such stock
> and vote thereon."
>
> — 8 Del. C. § 217(a), https://delcode.delaware.gov/title8/c001/sc07/index.html

> "(a) One stockholder or 2 or more stockholders may by agreement in writing
> deposit capital stock of an original issue with or transfer capital stock to
> any person or persons ... authorized to act as trustee, for the purpose of
> vesting in such person or persons ... who may be designated **voting trustee**
> ... the right to vote thereon for any period of time determined by such
> agreement ..."
>
> — 8 Del. C. § 218(a)

so run the three checks, in order, and **each is a fact about your own cap
table** rather than a statute you must go read:

| check | if NO | if YES |
|---|---|---|
| is any share held **in a fiduciary capacity** — a trustee, an executor, a custodian? | ✅ § 217(a)'s first sentence is vacuous | the records must show the fiduciary as the record holder |
| is any share **pledged**, with the pledgee expressly empowered on the books to vote? | ✅ § 217(a)'s second sentence is vacuous | ⚠️ **only the pledgee may vote** — the books must record that transfer |
| is any share subject to a **voting trust** or a written voting agreement? | ✅ § 218 is vacuous | a copy must sit at the registered office, open to inspection |

⭐ **a single-founder corporation answers NO three times**, which makes (ii)'s
evaluable half satisfied outright. that is a common case and it is worth two
minutes to confirm rather than assume.

⚠️ **a YES on any row does not defeat the dashboard** — it tells you the
dashboard must **record** that fact for the ledger to be complete. the § 219(c)
exclusivity above bites on voting rights, and every one of these three rows is a
voting-rights fact.

🎯 **the practical move is unchanged and now correctly grounded:** print the
dashboard's records to legible paper form, per § 224, and treat that as the
record. if the service kept no such record at all, **then** open a ledger — but
do not manufacture one to replace evidence you already hold.

### ⭐ why a certified charter copy CANNOT answer the issued-share question — § 105

the reflex when a state asks you to substantiate a share count is to order a
**certified copy** of the charter. it is official, it is stamped, and it is the
document Delaware itself holds. it will not answer.

§ 105 says exactly how far a certified copy reaches:

> "A copy of a certificate of incorporation ... shall, when duly certified by the
> Secretary of State, be received in all courts, public offices and official
> bodies as **prima facie evidence of**:
>
> (1) Due execution, acknowledgment and filing of the instrument;
>
> (2) Observance and performance of all acts and conditions necessary to have
> been observed and performed precedent to the instrument becoming effective; and
>
> (3) **Any other facts required or permitted by law to be stated in the
> instrument**."
>
> — 8 Del. C. § 105, https://delcode.delaware.gov/title8/c001/sc01/index.html

⛔ **clause (3) is the whole limit: a certified copy proves what the charter was
required to SAY.** so read what a charter is required to say:

> "(a) The certificate of incorporation **shall set forth**: ...
>
> (4) **If the corporation is to be authorized to issue only 1 class of stock**,
> the total number of shares of stock which the corporation shall have authority
> to issue and the par value of each of such shares, or a statement that all such
> shares are to be without par value. **If the corporation is to be authorized to
> issue more than 1 class of stock**, the certificate of incorporation shall set
> forth the total number of shares of all classes of stock which the corporation
> shall have authority to issue **and the number of shares of each class** and
> shall specify each class the shares of which are to be without par value and
> each class the shares of which are to have par value and the par value of the
> shares of each such class ..."
>
> — 8 Del. C. § 102(a)(4), https://delcode.delaware.gov/title8/c001/sc01/index.html

⚠️ **quote the fork, not one branch of it.** (4) is a conditional that splits on
one-class versus many-class, and the second branch demands **more** — a per-class
breakdown. neither branch asks for issued shares, so the conclusion below holds
either way; but a reader whose charter has two classes needs to see the branch
that governs them.

| the fact | required in the charter? | does a certified copy prove it? |
|---|---|---|
| **authorized** shares | ✅ § 102(a)(4) | ✅ yes |
| **par value** | ✅ § 102(a)(4) | ✅ yes |
| number of **classes** | ✅ § 102(a)(4) | ✅ yes |
| ⭐ **ISSUED shares** | ⛔ **absent from the whole of § 102(a)** | ⛔ **no** |

⭐ **that gap is not an oversight — it is structural.** authorized shares are a
**charter** term, fixed at formation and changed only by amendment. issued shares
are a **ledger** fact that moves every time the board acts. a document filed once
cannot testify to a number that changes afterward.

🎯 **so when the state asks you to substantiate issued shares, it asks for a fact
the charter was never able to supply**, and an officer's sworn statement — backed
by the board consent, the purchase agreement, and the § 224 records above — is
the instrument that fits. that is why the ask arrives as an **attestation**
rather than as a request for a certified copy.

⚠️ **do not read this as a licence to assert a number.** § 105 tells you which
document is the wrong tool; the count still comes off the records, and the
§ 502(b) perjury caution above is untouched.

### ⛔ if you don't know gross assets — do NOT estimate it

gross assets is **defined by statute as a number already reported to the IRS**,
not as a sum you assemble:

> "Such total assets and total gross assets shall be those "total assets"
> reported to the United States on U.S. Form 1120 Schedule L, relative to the
> company's fiscal year ending in the calendar year prior to filing with the
> Secretary of State pursuant to this section."
>
> — 8 Del. C. § 503(i)

> 🚩 **corrected 2026-09-14** — the same defect this route carries in
> `3.pay.taxes.stone`, and finding it in **two** files is what makes it a class
> rather than a slip. the statute's **double** quotes around "total assets" had
> been swapped for **single** ones because the block is itself wrapped in `"`.
> the quote also opened mid-sentence, with the statute's "Such" dropped and no
> ellipsis to mark the cut, and stopped before "pursuant to this section."

⛔ **and Delaware will check your figure against that exact page.** the standard
recalculation path ends with the state's request for a **signed Form 1120** —
Schedule L is the one page it reads. an estimate that does not match the line you
later hand them is a discrepancy you created yourself, in the document that was
supposed to settle the matter.

| your situation | what to enter |
|---|---|
| the 1120 for that year is filed | the **Schedule L "total assets"** figure. nothing else |
| the 1120 is not filed yet | ⛔ **stop.** file it first — you need it for the recalculation anyway |
| Schedule L was left blank (permitted under $250k) | ⚠️ fill it in. blank is legal for the IRS and **useless to Delaware**, which reads that page |
| Schedule L carries **zeros**, and Schedule K question 13 is checked **"Yes"** | 🚨 **treat the zeros as UNVERIFIED** — see below. a blank-only rule misses this case, and it is the commoner one |

#### 🚨 a ZEROED Schedule L is not a completed one — and it does not announce itself

the row above catches a **blank** page. it does not catch the commoner outcome:
a preparer takes the IRS exemption and the software writes **`0`** into the lines
rather than leave them empty.

⛔ **a blank tells you it was skipped. a zero tells you no such thing** — it is
byte-identical to a corporation that genuinely held no assets.

the tell sits on a different schedule:

> "If “Yes,” the corporation is not required to complete Schedules L, M-1, and
> M-2."
>
> — Form 1120, Schedule K, question 13,
> https://www.irs.gov/pub/irs-pdf/f1120.pdf
>
> bytes at
> `briefs/incorporation/variant=c-corp/refs/effectiveAt=2025-01-01.irs-f1120.txt`

> ✅ **the bytes now ship — closed.** this passage carried a declared
> ABSENT-SOURCE gap for part of a day: the quote is from an **IRS** form and
> every other embedded source on this route is a Delaware statute, so a census
> over them reported it **NOT-IN-SOURCE**. ⛔ that was never a licence to widen
> the detector or to delete the quotation — the cure for an absent source is to
> go get the source.
>
> 🚩 **the capture is the 2025 revision, and the question-13 threshold has moved
> before.** check the revision that governs *your* tax year before you lean on
> the $250,000 figure — a quote from the wrong year's form is the stale-revision
> defect, and it wears a citation that reads correct.

⇒ **read question 13 before you trust line 15.** if it is checked *"Yes"*, that
Schedule L was **never required**, so the figure may be a placeholder no one
computed — and it is the number Delaware reads and the number you later attest to
under § 502(b).

| Schedule K q.13 | what line 15 is worth |
|---|---|
| **"No"** — L was required | ✅ a computed balance. use it |
| **"Yes"** — L was exempt | ⚠️ possibly a placeholder. reconcile against bank and asset records **before** you state it |

🎯 **and for a year you have not filed yet, do not take the exemption.** it is a
permission, never a prohibition. Schedule L is the one page Delaware reads — take
the IRS's offer and you file a return whose sole Delaware-relevant page is empty.

⚠️ this governs what you may **say**, not what you **owe**. where assets sit far
below the sensitivity band from step 1, the figure cannot move the tax either
way — but § 503(b) still requires a complete and correct report.

⚠️ a bank balance, an equipment tally, or an IP valuation is **a different
number** than Schedule L total assets, and the difference is not conservative in
either direction — it is simply unverifiable.

### 🚩 "the 1120 for that year" is a GLOSS — and for a late filer it is WRONG

read the row above against the statute quoted 13 lines up. the row says *"for
that **year**"* and the statute does not say that:

> "relative to the company's fiscal year ending in the calendar year prior to
> **filing** with the Secretary of State pursuant to this section"
>
> — 8 Del. C. § 503(i)

⭐ **the anchor keys on the FILING, not on the report's tax year.** those are
the same date for a filer who files on time, and they are the whole problem for
a filer who does not:

| who you are | the report's tax year | "the calendar year prior to filing" | agree? |
|---|---|---|---|
| on time — the 2022 report, filed Feb 2023 | 2022 | 2022 | ✅ yes |
| ⛔ **delinquent** — the 2022 report, filed 2026 | 2022 | **2025** | ⛔ **no, by four years** |

so the literal text points a late filer at a fiscal year with no bearing on the
report they are amending. **do not settle that by reasoning, and do not assume
the intuitive answer.** this whole route turns on the fact that the intuitive
reading of a franchise-tax provision is the expensive one.

⚠️ **and the wrong figure here is a SWORN number.** § 502(b) puts the officer
under penalty of perjury and § 504(d) gives the Secretary of State inquiry power
over *"the truth or falsity or accuracy of every report."* a number keyed to the
wrong fiscal year is not an arithmetic slip — it is an attestation with the
wrong document behind it.

🎯 **so gather what you can substantiate, and carry the question forward.** the
ambiguity is on the call list in `2.call.delaware.stone`; the full read of (i)
is in `3.pay.taxes.stone`. do not pick a year here.

#### ✅ RESOLVED — the Division publishes its own gloss, and it is the intuitive one

> ⭐ the section above says **do not settle this by thought alone**. it does not
> say leave it open. the agency that administers the portal had already published
> its own answer, on a page this repo already cites six times — and nobody had
> looked.

> "Total Gross Assets shall be those “total assets” reported on the U.S. Form
> 1120, Schedule L (Federal Return) **relative to the company’s fiscal year
> ending the calendar year of the report**."
>
> — Delaware Division of Corporations, *How to Calculate Franchise Taxes*,
> https://corp.delaware.gov/frtaxcalc/
>
> pulled with bhrowser · bytes at
> `briefs/incorporation/variant=c-corp/refs/pulledAt=2026-08-26.de-frtaxcalc.txt`

⛔ **the two texts genuinely conflict**, and the conflict only appears for a
delinquent filer:

| source | the anchor it names | a 2023 report, amended in 2026, points at |
|---|---|---|
| **§ 503(i)** — the statute | the calendar year prior to when you file | fiscal year **2025** |
| ⭐ **the Division** — the agency | the calendar year **of the report** | fiscal year **2023** |

✅ **take the report's own tax year.** two reasons, and neither is a guess:

1. the Division runs the portal you will type the number into, so its published
   gloss is what the form is built around and what the clerk checks against
2. a number keyed to the report's own year is defensible on its face — an
   examiner who disagrees sees an honest read of the agency's own instruction,
   not an evasion

⚠️ **still put it to the clerk on the call.** you now hold both texts, so the
question is no longer open-ended: *"your calculator page says the fiscal year of
the report — the statute says the year prior to when I file. for an amended 2023
report submitted in 2026, which Schedule L do you want?"* that is a 20-second
question with a citation behind each option.

🚩 **and note what this does NOT unblock.** where a year's federal return is not
yet on file, there is no Schedule L for that year to read from. that is a
**form** gate under § 503(b) — the report demands a figure — and, where the
assumed-par tax sits on the $400.00 minimum across the whole sensitivity band
from step 1, not a **money** gate. so ask the clerk what they will accept on the
amended report in the meantime, rather than treat it as a hard stop.

### ✅ and the figure you hand them is NOT public record — § 502(e)

> ⭐ the natural objection to *"hand Delaware your balance
> sheet"* is that an annual report is public record. § 502(e) says both things,
> in two sentences, and the second is the carve-out.

> "The Secretary of State shall **safely keep all reports** returned in such
> manner as they may be **open to the inspection of all persons** pursuant to
> the provisions set forth in Chapter 100 of Title 29. **Any tax information
> provided pursuant to paragraph (a)(7) of this section, contained on annual
> franchise tax reports filed after tax year 2006 shall not be deemed
> public.**"
>
> — 8 Del. C. § 502(e), https://delcode.delaware.gov/title8/c005/index.html

> 🚩 **a COMMA was added to that quote until 2026-09-14, and it was not
> cosmetic.** the artifact read "…filed after tax year 2006**,** shall not be
> deemed public." the statute has no comma there.
>
> ⛔ **the mark changes the grammar, and the grammar is the scope.** with that
> comma, "contained on annual franchise tax reports filed after tax year 2006"
> becomes a **non-restrictive** aside and the subject is *any* (a)(7) tax
> information — a broader shield. with no comma it is **restrictive**, and only
> (a)(7) information that sits on a **post-2006 report** is withheld.
>
> ⚠️ the legislature left that phrase unclosed, so the ambiguity is theirs. our
> punctuation resolved it silently, in our own favour, inside quotation marks.
> ⭐ **a typographic tidy-up of a statute is an interpretive act** — and the one
> defect class no reader ever checks for, because a stray comma reads as noise.
>
> the table below reasons from the **restrictive** read, which is the narrower
> of the two and the one the bytes support.

| what | public? |
|---|---|
| the annual franchise tax **report** itself | ✅ open to inspection |
| **tax information** supplied under **§ 502(a)(7)**, on reports after **tax year 2006** | ⛔ **not deemed public** |

⭐ **§ 502(a)(7) is the catch-all the Division uses to demand extras** — *"Such
additional information, schedules and attachments as the Secretary shall
require."* so the attachments they ask you for land in the **non-public** bucket
by the same subsection that lets them ask.

⚠️ **read the two limits before you rely on it.** the carve-out covers *"tax
information ... **contained on** annual franchise tax reports"* — so its clean
case is a schedule furnished **with a report** under (a)(7). whether a document
sent by **email to an analyst**, outside a report, sits inside that phrase is
**not settled by this text**. if confidentiality matters to you, **ask** — and
prefer the attachment path over the inbox.

⛔ and note what it does **not** do: it is a public-records rule, not a shield
against the Secretary. § 504(d) still allows inquiry into *"the truth or falsity
or accuracy of every report"* and production of the corporation's books.

### ⛔ if you don't know par value — do NOT assume the startup default

> 🚩 **this section used to say "usually $0.0001 for startups." corrected
> 2026-09-02.** it was the third default guess in the table above, on the third
> of three fields, and all three feed the same calculator and the same annual
> report.

par value is not a convention. it is **a term of your charter**, stated verbatim
on the Certificate of Incorporation, and it varies — $0.0001 and $0.00001 are
both common, and plenty of charters carry $0.01, $0.10, or $1.00.

⚠️ **the number goes on the annual report**, which carries 8 Del. C. § 502(b)
perjury, and into `franchise.tax.delaware --par` in step 1. a value you assumed
is a false statement whether or not it changes the money.

> ⚠️ unlike issued shares and gross assets, par is **not** on step 2's
> what-to-have-ready list — the clerk recalculates, and the charter term reaches
> the state on the amended report rather than over the phone. that makes it the
> quietest of the three, not the least binding.

#### does a wrong par change the tax?

measured with `franchise.tax.delaware`, at 10,000,000 authorized / 8,000,000
issued / $50 gross assets:

| stated par | assumed-par capital | annual tax |
|---|---:|---:|
| $0.0001 | $1,000.00 | **$400.00** |
| $0.00001 | $100.00 | **$400.00** |
| $0.50 | $5,000,000.00 | **$2,000.00** |

so a factor-of-ten slip between the two startup values is **inert** — both land
on the **§ 503(c)** $400 minimum. but a charter whose real par is materially
larger is a different tax entirely, and the guess **understates** it, which is
the direction the state cares about.

⛔ read it off the charter. if you cannot find the charter, order a certified
copy from the Division of Corporations before this call — the same office you
phone in step 2.

